  "metaDescription": "Fidelity alternatives compared: Schwab, Merrill Edge, E*TRADE, Robinhood, Webull, Interactive Brokers and Ally Invest by option, bond and margin fees.",
  "published": "2026-11-22",
  "updated": "2026-11-22",
  "intro": "Charles Schwab, Merrill Edge, E*TRADE, Robinhood, Webull, Interactive Brokers and Ally Invest are seven Fidelity alternatives for a brokerage account. Most of them also list $0 online commissions on United States (US) stocks and exchange-traded funds (ETFs), so the differences sit in option and bond fees, margin rates, minimums, transfer fees and robo-advisor pricing. The table below lists each broker's published fees, and the sections after it run the yearly math on contracts, bonds and margin balances.\n\n[Fidelity](https://www.fidelity.com/) lists $0 account fees and $0 commissions on online US stock, ETF and option trades, with option contract fees applying on top. Fidelity has no minimum to open an account and sells fractional shares of US stocks and ETFs for as little as $1. Account types include a brokerage account, a Roth individual retirement account (IRA), a health savings account (HSA) and a 529 account for education savings.\n\nFidelity's margin rate builds on a base rate of 10.825%, effective September 18, 2026, and a debit balance under $25,000 pays 12.075%. Secondary-market bonds cost $1.00 each online. Non-Fidelity mutual funds that carry a transaction fee cost $49.95 per purchase.\n\nFor a head-to-head view, see the [Robinhood and Fidelity comparison](/compare/robinhood-vs-fidelity/). The guide to [what brokers charge to trade](/guides/what-do-brokers-charge-to-trade/) explains each fee type, and the [Robinhood alternatives](/alternatives/robinhood/) page lists brokers compared against Robinhood. More breakdowns sit in the [comparison hub](/compare/).",
