  "chooseSection": {
    "heading": "How to Choose a Fidelity Alternative",
    "body": "Fidelity and all seven alternatives list $0 commissions on online US stock trades, with Interactive Brokers limiting that price to IBKR Lite for US residents. The stock commission does not separate them. Compare these five costs instead.\n\n- Option contracts. Schwab and E*TRADE list $0.65 per contract, and E*TRADE drops to 50 cents at 30 or more trades per quarter. Fidelity and Merrill Edge say contract fees apply without listing an amount on the pages checked.\n- Bonds. Fidelity charges $1.00 per secondary bond online. Schwab and E*TRADE charge $1 per bond with a $10 minimum, so a 5-bond order costs $5 at Fidelity and $10 at Schwab or E*TRADE.\n- Margin. Fidelity charges 12.075% on debit balances from $0 to $24,999. Interactive Brokers lists margin loans starting at 4.38%.\n- Minimums. Schwab, Merrill Edge, Webull and Interactive Brokers list no minimum. Ally self-directed trading starts at $0 and Robinhood at $1.\n- Moving money out. Merrill Edge charges $49.95 for a full account transfer and $24.95 per wire. Webull charges $75 for an outgoing stock transfer, and Fidelity lists $0 for a domestic bank wire.\n\nRobo pricing is the last check if a managed portfolio is part of the plan. Schwab Intelligent Portfolios charges no advisory fee with a $5,000 minimum. Ally Robo Portfolios need $100 and offer a no-advisory-fee option or 0.30% a year, while Fidelity does not publish a robo-advisor price on the pages that could be read."
  },
  "alternatives": [
    {
      "heading": "Charles Schwab Option and Bond Fees",
      "body": "Charles Schwab lists $0 online commissions on listed stocks and ETFs and charges $0.65 per option contract on top of a $0 base commission. Schwab has no account or trade minimums. Thousands of mutual funds in Schwab Mutual Fund OneSource carry $0 commission, and new-issue bonds and Treasuries cost $0 online.\n\nSecondary bonds, certificates of deposit (CDs) and corporate bonds cost $1 per bond online at Schwab, with a $10 minimum and a $250 maximum. Broker-assisted trades add a $25 service charge. A foreign stock bought on the US over-the-counter (OTC) market carries a $50 foreign transaction fee online.\n\nTen option contracts at $0.65 each cost $6.50 at Schwab. Twenty secondary-market bonds cost $20.00 at Schwab and $20.00 at Fidelity, because both charge $1 a bond once the order clears Schwab's minimum. The two split on a 5-bond order: $10 at Schwab against $5 at Fidelity.\n\nSchwab Intelligent Portfolios charges no advisory fee and no commissions, with a $5,000 minimum. ETF operating expenses and a cash allocation apply. Charles Schwab fits people who want a published per-contract option fee and a robo portfolio without an advisory fee.\n\nSchwab falls short of Fidelity on small bond orders and on representative help. Schwab adds $25 to a broker-assisted trade, while Fidelity charges $19.95 for a representative-assisted Treasury auction order."
    },
    {
      "heading": "Merrill Edge Transfer and Wire Fees",
      "body": "Merrill Edge lists $0 online stock, ETF and option trades in its Self-Directed general investing accounts, with no minimum investment. Merrill Edge says options contract and other fees may apply, but its pricing page does not give a per-contract figure. Merrill is part of Bank of America.\n\nThe Merrill Edge fee list centers on account services. A full account transfer costs $49.95, while a partial account transfer costs $0. Domestic and international wires cost $24.95 each, and the bond coupon fee is $5.\n\nWeb bill payment, a checkbook and unlimited check writing are $0 at Merrill Edge. Paying bills and writing checks from the account adds nothing to the yearly cost.\n\nCompare wires first. Fidelity lists $0 for a domestic bank wire, so four domestic wires a year cost $0 at Fidelity and 4 x $24.95 = $99.80 at Merrill Edge. Moving the whole account out of Merrill Edge costs $49.95, while a partial transfer costs $0.\n\nMerrill Edge fits people who want a brokerage from Bank of America with $0 check writing and bill payment. Merrill Edge falls short of Fidelity on wire fees. Neither Merrill Edge nor Fidelity lists a per-contract option fee, while Schwab and E*TRADE both list $0.65."
    },
    {
      "heading": "E*TRADE Option and Futures Pricing",
      "body": "E*TRADE lists $0 commission trades on stocks, options, mutual funds and ETFs. Option contracts cost $0.65 each, or 50 cents each with 30 or more trades per quarter. E*TRADE is from Morgan Stanley.\n\nE*TRADE also publishes futures and crypto prices, which the Fidelity pages checked do not list. Futures cost $1.50 per contract per side plus fees, and E-nano Equity Index futures cost $0.75. Crypto trades carry a commission of 0.50% of each trade's dollar value, and bonds cost $1.00 each with a $10 minimum and a $250 maximum.\n\nTen option contracts cost $6.50 at the standard rate and $5.00 at the 50-cent rate. One futures contract opened and then closed counts as two sides, so it costs 2 x $1.50 = $3.00 plus fees. A $1,000 crypto purchase costs 0.50% x $1,000 = $5.00 in commission.\n\nE*TRADE runs limited-time promotions for new accounts. Those offers change, so the cost math here leaves them out. E*TRADE fits people who place 30 or more trades a quarter or want futures pricing on a published schedule.\n\nE*TRADE matches Schwab on bonds and falls short of Fidelity on small bond orders. Five bonds cost $10 at E*TRADE and $5 at Fidelity."
    },
    {
      "heading": "Robinhood Gold Subscription Pricing",
      "body": "Robinhood describes itself as 24/5 commission-free stock trading and investing, and an account can start with as little as $1. Robinhood offers stocks, ETFs, crypto, options, futures and prediction markets. Other fees sit on Robinhood's fee schedule, which the homepage does not reproduce.\n\nRobinhood Gold is a subscription at $5 a month, which is 12 x $5 = $60 a year. Gold members pay zero management fees on every dollar over $100K in Robinhood Strategies, an expert-managed portfolio. Fidelity and the other six alternatives do not list a subscription on the pages checked.\n\nFidelity and Robinhood both list a $1 starting point. Robinhood uses it for opening an account, and Fidelity uses it for buying fractional shares of US stocks and ETFs.\n\nRobinhood fits people who want futures and prediction markets alongside stocks, ETFs, crypto and options. Robinhood falls short of Fidelity on published fee detail in the facts gathered here. Fidelity lists bond, mutual fund, margin and wire prices on its commission page, while Robinhood's homepage points to a separate fee schedule.\n\nThe [Robinhood and Fidelity comparison](/compare/robinhood-vs-fidelity/) and the [Robinhood alternatives](/alternatives/robinhood/) page cover Robinhood's pricing in more detail."
    },
    {
      "heading": "Webull Option Fees and Outgoing Transfers",
      "body": "Webull lists $0 commissions for stocks, ETFs and options listed on US exchanges, with no deposit minimums. Certain index option trades carry a $0.50 per contract fee, and regulatory pass-through fees apply. Moving stock into Webull costs $0, and an outgoing stock transfer costs $75.\n\nTen contracts on one of those index options cost 10 x $0.50 = $5.00 at Webull. The same count of standard contracts costs $6.50 at Schwab, and Fidelity lists contract fees without an amount. For other options listed on US exchanges, Webull's pricing page names $0 commissions plus regulatory pass-through fees.\n\nWebull states how it earns money: stock loans, interest on free credit balances, margin interest and payment for order flow.\n\nWebull fits people who want $0 option commissions with no deposit minimum. Webull falls short on leaving. Its $75 outgoing stock transfer is higher than the $49.95 Merrill Edge charges for a full account transfer, and the Fidelity pages checked do not list a transfer-out fee."
    },
    {
      "heading": "Interactive Brokers Margin Rates and Per-Share Pricing",
      "body": "Interactive Brokers lists margin loans starting at 4.38% and pays up to 3.38% on uninvested cash. Interactive Brokers lists no account minimums, ticket charges or platform fees on its commissions page.\n\nCommission pricing comes in two plans. IBKR Lite charges $0 on US stocks and ETFs, for US residents only. IBKR Pro Tiered charges $0.0035 per share at 300,000 shares a month or less, with a $0.35 minimum per order and a maximum of 1% of trade value.\n\nIBKR Pro Fixed charges $0.005 per share with a $1.00 minimum. A 1,000-share order costs 1,000 x $0.0035 = $3.50 on Pro Tiered and 1,000 x $0.005 = $5.00 on Pro Fixed. A 100-share order works out to $0.50 on Pro Fixed, so the $1.00 minimum applies.\n\nA $10,000 margin balance held for a year costs $1,207.50 at Fidelity's 12.075% tier and $438.00 at Interactive Brokers' 4.38% starting rate. That is a difference of $769.50 a year. The 4.38% figure is a starting rate, and other balances and currencies differ.\n\nInteractive Brokers fits people who carry a margin balance or want interest on uninvested cash. Interactive Brokers falls short of Fidelity on stock pricing outside IBKR Lite, because the Pro plans charge per share while Fidelity lists $0 online US stock commissions."
    },
    {
      "heading": "Ally Invest Robo Portfolios and Personal Advice",
      "body": "Ally Invest lists $0 to start for self-directed trading and no commission fees on most US-listed securities. Self-directed accounts cover stocks, bonds, ETFs and margin accounts.\n\nAlly Robo Portfolios need a $100 minimum. Ally offers two pricing choices: no advisory fee, or 0.30% a year in exchange for putting more of the portfolio in the market. On $10,000, the 0.30% choice costs 0.30% x $10,000 = $30 a year.\n\nAlly Personal Advice starts at $100,000 in assets under care, with a blended annual advisory fee of at most 0.85%. At $100,000, that ceiling works out to 0.85% x $100,000 = $850 a year.\n\nThe $100 Ally robo minimum is $4,900 lower than the $5,000 minimum for Schwab Intelligent Portfolios. Fidelity does not publish a robo-advisor price on the pages that could be read, so Ally and Schwab are the two brokers here with published robo minimums and fees. The [Wealthfront alternatives](/alternatives/wealthfront/) page compares more automated investing services.\n\nAlly Invest fits people who want a robo portfolio from $100 with a no-advisory-fee choice. Ally falls short of Fidelity on published trading detail, since the Ally page says most US-listed securities carry no commission and does not list the exceptions."
    }
  ],
  "stillBetterFit": {
    "heading": "When Fidelity Is Still the Better Fit",
    "body": "Fidelity fits people who want $0 account fees, no minimum to open and fractional shares of US stocks and ETFs from $1. Fidelity funds trade at $0, and Treasuries cost $0 at auction and on the secondary market online. New-issue bonds and CDs cost $0, and secondary bonds cost $1.00 each with no online minimum listed, so a 5-bond order costs less than at Schwab or E*TRADE.\n\nFidelity also lists $0 for domestic bank wires, the Fidelity Account ATM fee and real-time quotes. Brokerage, Roth IRA, HSA and 529 accounts sit under one name, and the Fidelity HSA carries no account fees or minimums. To model Roth IRA contributions, the [Roth IRA calculator](/investing/roth-ira-calculator/) runs the numbers.\n\nFidelity's margin tiers drop as the balance grows: 11.575% from $25,000 to $49,999, 10.625% from $50,000 to $99,999, 10.575% from $100,000 to $249,999 and 10.325% from $250,000 to $499,999. Fidelity describes its margin rates as 'among the most competitive in the industry'. Interactive Brokers lists a 4.38% starting rate.\n\nWho This Is Not For\n\n- Fidelity fits poorly for people carrying a margin balance under $25,000, who pay 12.075%. Interactive Brokers lists margin loans starting at 4.38%.\n- Fidelity fits poorly for people who want a published per-contract option fee, since the Fidelity pages checked list none. Schwab and E*TRADE list $0.65, and E*TRADE lists 50 cents at 30 or more trades per quarter.\n- Fidelity fits poorly for frequent buyers of non-Fidelity transaction-fee mutual funds, at $49.95 per purchase and up to $100 on some funds. Schwab Mutual Fund OneSource lists $0 on thousands of funds, and E*TRADE lists $0 commission on mutual funds.\n- Fidelity charges $49.95 when no transaction fee (NTF) non-Fidelity funds are sold within 60 days of purchase. Holding those funds past 60 days avoids that redemption fee.\n- Fidelity fits poorly for people who want published futures or prediction market pricing, which the Fidelity pages checked do not list. E*TRADE publishes futures prices, and Robinhood offers both futures and prediction markets.\n- Schwab Intelligent Portfolios fits poorly for balances under $5,000. Ally Robo Portfolios start at $100.\n\nWhat Would Change Our Answer\n\n- Fidelity publishing an option contract fee would allow a direct comparison with the $6.50 Schwab and E*TRADE charge for 10 contracts.\n- Fidelity moving its 10.825% margin base rate, or Interactive Brokers raising its 4.38% starting rate, would change the $769.50 yearly gap on a $10,000 balance.\n- Fidelity publishing a robo-advisor price would add a third published robo figure next to Schwab and Ally.\n- E*TRADE changing its threshold of 30 trades per quarter for 50-cent contracts.\n- Robinhood changing the $5 monthly Gold price.\n- Merrill Edge or Webull changing their $49.95 and $75 transfer-out fees."
  },
  "verdict": "Fidelity alternatives match Fidelity on online US stock commissions and part ways on the fees around them. Interactive Brokers lists margin loans from 4.38%, against 12.075% at Fidelity on balances under $25,000. E*TRADE lists 50 cents per option contract at 30 or more trades a quarter, and Ally Invest opens a robo portfolio at $100.\n\nCharles Schwab pairs a $0.65 contract fee with a no-advisory-fee robo portfolio. Merrill Edge adds $0 check writing and bill payment, Webull lists $0 option commissions with no deposit minimum, and Robinhood sells Gold at $5 a month. Fidelity holds the lead on $0 account fees, $0 domestic wires and $1.00 bonds with no online minimum listed.\n\nFor Fidelity alternatives, the concrete next step is to write down your yearly option contracts, bond orders and average margin balance, then price each one against the matching row in the table above.",
